10/28/2023
CS3500 HW5
Flipping the Script: Reversi
Mathew Ambrose and Sophia Yang
Overview:
This project is a project that allows for the play of a game of Reversi. In the current version,
we have implemented a BasicReversi model class, which implements a higher level ReversiModel
interface which holds public methods that are necessary for the user to be able to access to play.
This also ensures that in the situation that different types of Reversi game versions may need to
be implemented in the future, they will be able to implement the existing interface instead of
changing the current BasicReversi class. We also envisioned for there to be more than just a
textual view, so our textual view that is currently being used to represent the model implements
another higher level interface IView which will end up storing more views as needed.

In the current working implementation of BasicReversi, we have the model constructor take in a final
private int representing the board's size.
By doing so, we can make many the invariants of the board being immutable, and constant.
For example: "The size of the board will always be greater than or equal to 5".
This is an invariant because
  1. It is a logic statement, and can be answered as true to our model.
  2. It is ensured by the constructor, as in the constructor there is a helper method called which
     throws an IllegalArgumentException if the size constructed is less than 5.
  3. It is ensured by the methods, as no method is ever allowed to change the size of the board
     by calling "final" on it.
another higher level interface IView which will end up storing more views as needed. To handle our
Reversi games' interactions with classes that need to access it, such as the view, we decided to
make a read only version of the interface which stores the observational methods of Reversi games.
This way, the view, or any other future class that may need a delegate of the model, is not allowed
to mutate the model as they will only be able to access the read only version.

Quickstart:
Currently, the way that we test the code, is to first instantiate a model, then call methods on it
to run through actions that an active game may play.
For example:
ReversiModel model = new BasicReversi(5);
model.makePlay(0, 1);
To visually test if this move was correctly made, we implemented a ReversiTextualView that allows
the current state of the game to be written out as an ASCII image in the Build Output.
For example:
TextualView view = new ReversiTextualView(model);
view.toString();
This will output the current ASCII view of the model like so:
  _ _ _
 _ X O _
_ O _ X _
 _ X O _
  _ _ _
Source organization:
Key components:
The key components of the current reversi game is the Main method, the ReversiModel interface, and
the TextualView interface. These three methods in theory will eventually work together to create a
playable Reversi game (along with some other interfaces such as GUI and Controller). Currently, the
Main method passes a model into the view, which allows the view to draw an accurate textual
representation of the model. The model though, is what drives changes in the view. The view cannot
modify or access any void methods in the model.
Key subcomponents:
In the model, the most prevalent terms must be the constructor for board, which is a 2D array with
rows that are ascending in order until the middle row, then descend to make a hexagon shape. The
most prevalent method is makePlay(int x, int y), which allows the user to make a move on the
hexagon array by given coordinate, as well as isGameOver which determines if the game is finished
or not. For now, the most important component of the view is the toString() method, which renders
the current board of the model as a string concatenation.



11/14/2023
CS3500 HW6
Flipping the Script: Reversi Part 2
Mathew Ambrose and Sophia Yang

Overview and Changes for Part 2:
In this version of our Reversi implementation, we made some great changes to our model. First,
we abstracted our previous model implementation into an AbstractReversi class, which allowed us to
easily implement and reuse as much code as we could for our BasicReversiWithAi class. Next, we
implemented a GUI, and a mock/stub controller allowing us to test the functionality of the GUI,
and the feature to see every time a cell is selected/deselected. Since the GUI is navigated through
mouse input, there is no conversion or shift for the logical coordinates of the array. Finally,
we implemented a mock ReversiAi class, that keeps track and makes sure all of the possible positions
are correctly made.

Extra credit:
Our extra credit implementation for the Reversi AI stacked strategies lies in the strategy folder.
Here, we implemented several different classes representing different strategies the AI can choose
from, and then a combining class where methods containing stacking and choosing the best strategy
can be found. The BasicReversiAi class is mainly different from the higher level abstract class in
the addition of the method “aiTurnToChooseBestStrategy”. This method chooses the best strategy for
the Reversi Ai to play. We write this by creating an int array containing all the different
strategy coordinates possible to play. The priority goes: minimizing opponent’s streaks ->
playing corners -> playing spots three away from a corner -> playing the sides while staying away
from spots next to the corners. The default move if no other moves work is a move that maximizes
score. If no moves are available, the Ai will pass their turn.

Quickstart/How to Play:
Go to the reversi folder, and run the Reversi file. This is our current main implementation.
Mouse clicks will select a cell, and clicking on the same one again will deselect it. On a selected
cell, click ENTER to make that play, or the P key to pass.

Source organization =>
Key components:
The key components in our new version are our GUI/ReversiView interface and our BasicReversiAi
class.

The ReversiView takes in a read-only version of the model, and constructs a view based on the
current state of the game. This is done within the class ReversiFrame, which extends JFrame and
implements the view interface.

The main methods for ReversiView are makeVisible, which makes the board visible in the window,
refresh, which refreshes the view every time an action is performed, and addClickListener, which
adds a mouse listener to the view.

Key subcomponents:
As described above, all key components of the Ai are contained in BasicReversiAi and the strategies
folder.

The key subcomponents of the ReversiView are:
HexagonPanel:
	HexagonPanel extends JPanel, and is an object within the BasicReversiFrame. HexagonPanel
	features a few essential methods and components, one being the Hexagon static class.

	Hexagon extends JPanel, and creates a new Path2D that draws a hexagon shape with the given hexagon
	size.

	Next, in the drawGrid method, a copy of the current board is iterated through every item in the
	array which represents the board, and draws a hexagon in that spot. If the hexagon is occupied by
	a player, it draws a circle in a color corresponding to their player type (i.e. BLACK gets a red
	circle, WHITE gets a green circle).

	We implemented a private MouseInputAdapter class that gets the physical points at which a user
	clicks the GUI. This is converted to the logical coordinates of the board first by storing all the
	physical points of each Hexagon drawn inside of a map, and then mapping each hexagon to its own
	logical coordinate as well. This information is iterated through a for loop, and the physical and
	logical coordinates are subsequently linked to each other through the object of their hexagons in
	both maps.

	There is a field in our HexagonPanel class that stores the logical coordinates of the current
	hexagon being considered by the player called selectedHexagon. This field is updated in the mouse
	adapter class, by setting it as the logical coordinate being clicked on, or resetting the field
	into a null array if the same hexagon is clicked again. This is also used in the drawGrid method,
	and draws a yellow hexagon at the point which the current selectedHexagon is.

	We also have a KeyInputAdapter class that allows for key presses to be read. The field
	selectedHexagon is yet again passed into this static class, and passes that information back to
	the controller so the controller can make a move at the specified logical coordinates.



11/30/2023
CS3500 HW7
Flipping the Script: Reversi Part 3
Mathew Ambrose and Sophia Yang

Overview and Changes for Part 3:
Fixes:
  We implemented some fixes from the previous assignment. Now, our view, is able to be resized
  according to the window.

In part 3 of the assignment, we have introduced two new essential components to enhance
communication between the Model and Player through the Controller.

  1. Model Listener Interface: This interface provides a set of features and methods that allow the
  model to communicate with other components of the system, specifically the Player. It serves
  as an interface for the Model to send relevant updates and information to the Controller, which
  then notifies the Player when it is their turn. When the Player has made their turn, it notifies
  the controller which notifies the Model that a turn has been made, and action has been performed.
  Now the model can and will go and notify the next controller that their turn is available.

  2. Player/View Listener Interface: This class complements the Model Listener by offering a way for
  the Player to interact with the Controller through the GUI. It provides methods and features
  that facilitate the exchange of information from the GUI to the Controller.

To explain the overall flow, first, when the controllers are initialized, they take in a model. This
instance of the model, once having startGame on it, will subscribe this controller to itself inside
of a list of controllers, and all subsequent controllers that use this same model as listeners.

To run the game, startGame in the model is called. startGame not only sets up the controllers as
listeners, but also at the end kicks off the game by having the first player, player X or Black,
notified that it is their turn.

Inside the controller, the method for notifying the move to the player features a while loop that
waits for the player to move. Once the player has made a move, the while loop breaks, and the
controller then communicates back to the model that a move has been made by the player.

Now that the model has been notified a move has been made, the model then calls on the next
controller in the list of subscribed controllers to notify their player that it is their turn to
move. This process continues on until the game has ended.

Moves made by the player are made through the Player interacting with the GUI, and thus emit the
Player actions through the GUI. The GUI then communicates to the Controller which move the Player
wants to make, and the GUI mutates the model as accordingly. After any action a player may perform,
the while loop is broken, and the model is notified that it is turn to notify the next player.

Quickstart/How to Play:
Run the Jar file with your desired arguments. The first two arguments must be the player type:
either human or ai. Our game of reversi automatically instantiates with a standard 11 by 11 board.
In the event that there is an ai, you must specify what mode the ai plays: easystrategy,
normalstrategy, or hardstrategy. This will determine how sophisticated the strategies of the ai are.
The easy strategy is just choosing any move, a regular strategy will prioritize corner pieces, and
a hard strategy will actively make moves to. The strategies are stacked when instantiated in the
main method.

Mouse clicks will select a cell, and clicking on the same one again, or clicking outside the area of
the board will deselect it. Clicking on a cell while another cell is selected with deselect the
previous cell and select the cell you clicked on. Cells with disks already placed on them are not
allowed to be selected, as you cannot make a play in a spot where another player has already made a
play.

On a selected cell, click ENTER to make that play, or the P key to pass. These moves will only be
allowed if the current view corresponds to the player whose turn it is. Otherwise, a message will be
displayed telling the player it is not their turn currently. What the player can do in this time, is
select and deselect cells on the board regardless of whether it is their turn or not. The only thing
impermissible is acting on those cells.

Source organization =>
Key components:
The key components in our new version are our GUIFeatures and ModelFeatures interfaces.

The GUIFeatures interface holds all the actions which a player can perform, and these actions are
administered through the GUI, as all player interactions (key and mouse presses) are through the
view.

The ModelFeatures interface holds methods that are used to keep track of whether or not a move has
been made by a player.

Both of these interfaces are featured in the controller, allowing for communication between model
and player through the controller.

Key subcomponents:
The key subcomponents in GUIFeatures and ModelFeatures are their methods, and implementations.

In GUIFeatures, there is the method to makeMove or pass. These are registered actions which once
are heard by the controller listener within the view their keyboard commands being pressed will
notify the model that a move is trying to be made.

In ModelFeatures, there are also two methods, which is notifyMove an notifyTurnMade. These two
methods communicate back and forth throughout the Model, Controller then Player (as well as the
other way around).

